There are two distinct systems involved here.

The original system, NitroHack's, saved the original RNG state, plus all
commands sent to the game (that had any effect, rather than simply
giving the user information), as well as the RNG state every turn;
loading a file involved using a binary save if available, or otherwise
replaying the game from the start (which can take tens of minutes).
The huge problem with such a system is that any change to the gameplay
at all causes saves to no longer load correctly, as does anything that
depended on an uninitialized variable or similar dubious piece of
memory. The saving of the RNG state every turn was done to detect that
this had happened, although NitroHack had no way to continue at that
point but deleting the save file.

I considered this unacceptable for NetHack 4; so I supplemented the
existing NitroHack save system with a system of my own, that stores the
entire gamestate every turn (using heavy compression, including binary
diffs, so that most turns only lead to a few tens or hundreds of bytes
of information). That way, even if the game engine is changed, as long
as the binary format used for the gamestate doesn't change, old save
files can be loaded (and even replayed, via simply replaying messages
and loading gamestate after gamestate and displaying them to the screen,
without the game engine being run at all). This format is pretty
disk-hungry, even with modern disk sizes; apart from that, the problems
with it appear to be bugs rather than failures of the underlying model
(in particular, files being overwritten in the wrong places, or an
incorrect number of newlines being output in the file, that sort of
thing). The other problem is that the binary save code that's being used
to produce the diffs appears to not store all the information in the
game (we haven't been able to track down the errors concretely or they'd
have been fixed, but one known symptom is the "zombie shopkeeper" bug
where shopkeepers in bones seem to be inconsistent as to whether they
act like they're in charge of their shop or not).

Apart from the binary save fixes, the save fixes that NetHack 4 needs,
therefore, are just some invariants to know things like where the file
pointer should be in the file at a particular point in the code, whether
newlines/spaces have been output yet or still need outputting, and so
on; and changing the code to conform to that. The locking system, and
possibly server code, also seem to be broken. (The code's in the state
it is because it attempted to build on the existing NitroHack code,
rather than rewriting it.)

